# THE DIVE'S LAUNCH DECK — EDIT ME (owner 2026-08-30: "you can just plan it out
# with ascii characters like we've done with the ship builder").
#
# This is the ground a run starts on. It spawns FROZEN at depth 1, like a nest,
# and the game reads the BERTHS straight out of it: every run of PLATFORM cells
# wide enough to hold a hull becomes a berth, and a candidate ship is moored a
# short drop below its centre. Move a platform here and the ship moves with it.
#
#   #  hull      — the WALKWAY. Solid, continuous, walk the whole deck on it.
#   -  platform  — a DROP-THROUGH hatch, and a BERTH: a ship hangs under it.
#   .  empty     — the air under a hatch, so the ship has somewhere to be.
#
# The owner drew this layout (2026-08-30): "which would allow for ships to be a
# block or two BELOW the platforms so they're easy to reach and you can just
# keep walking to the ship you want/have unlocked... and also the platforms can
# be as wide as whatever ship is right under it plus a small buffer of 2 blocks."
# So the deck has no GAPS to jump across any more — it is one unbroken floor,
# and choosing a ship is S+jump through the hatch above it. Walk off either END
# and there is nothing under you at all, which is the run with no ship.
#
# SCALE — READ THIS BEFORE YOU RESIZE A PLATFORM. This blueprint is UPSCALED by
# the world scale (8), so ONE CHARACTER HERE IS 8 × Ship.CELL = 128 WORLD PIXELS.
# The player is 80×144 px, so a character is a block a bit wider than a person.
#
# THE HULLS ARE NOT ALL AUTHORED AT THE SAME SCALE, and sizing a platform by
# counting a hull's columns is how this deck came out FOUR TIMES TOO WIDE
# (2026-08-30). Measure in PIXELS:
#
#   ships/starter.ship   is NATIVE 8× — never upscaled — so its 96 columns are
#                        96 × Ship.CELL = 1,536 px =  12 characters here.
#   ships/loft_test.ship IS upscaled 8× on spawn, so its 22 columns are
#                        22 × 8 × Ship.CELL = 2,816 px = 22 characters here.
#
# A deck sized off "96 columns must be 96 characters" gave the starter a berth
# 14,080 px wide for a 1,536 px ship — so walking off the platform edge dropped
# you into open air a whole screen from the hull, and you fell past everything
# (owner: "player drops like a METEOR when getting on the ship").
#
# In CHARACTERS the sizes are the same at every world scale, which is why this
# file counts in them: the legacy 1× scene flies a 12-column starter (192 px at
# a 16 px cell) and a 1×-spawned Loft of 22 columns (352 px), so both hulls are
# still 12 and 22 characters of deck. Size a platform in characters and it is
# right at both scales; size it in pixels and it is right at neither.
#
# The layout below, left to right:
#
#   12 walkway  |  14 hatch (the 12-wide starter)  |  14 walkway  |
#   24 hatch (the 22-wide Loft ship)  |  12 walkway
#
# THE HATCH IS EXACTLY HULL + 2 CHARACTERS (DiveDeck.BERTH_BUFFER_CELLS), which
# is the owner's number and is the tightest it can be: the hull rises out
# through its own hatch when you take it, and needs that block either side to
# clear the walkway. It is also the LOOSEST it should be — a hatch wider than
# its hull is a place you can drop through and miss, and the headless probe
# lands you on the hull from the middle of a hatch and drops you 38,000 px past
# it from the lip of one four characters too wide.
#
# So a hatch is not a free size. Widen a hull and this file must follow, and the
# unit suite fails loudly (both directions) rather than letting a run quietly
# moor a candidate off the end of the deck.
#
# Only the TOP row is platform: a hatch is one surface per storey, not a stack
# of planks (ShipLayout.upscale_cells keeps PLATFORM one row deep), and stacked
# strips would need one S+jump each. The rows under it are empty air.
#
# 'origin COL ROW' names which character is deck cell (0,0) — put it in the
# middle of the MIDDLE walkway, because that is where the run stands you.

origin 33 0
############--------------##############------------------------############
############..............##############........................############
############..............##############........................############
